WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.150
This video was made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:09.900
Subscribe to their Nebula bundle deal at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover
to watch an exclusive companion video to this.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:15.510
It’s really just four lanes, a median, and
some on and off ramps.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:22.279
The Interstate Highway system is no engineering
marvel, no unprecedented undertaking, no massively

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:23.539
innovative concept.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:28.980
It’s a simple idea—one that arose since
the earliest days of the automobile.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.470
It’s not exciting, which is why it often
goes so under appreciated.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:39.060
Take the Interstates away, though, and you
have a weaker nation.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.900
You have one with dramatically starker regional
divides, one with increased decentralization—more

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:50.970
specifically, you have a nation absent of
a noticeable portion of the late 1900s economic

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.600
development and transformation.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.260
These words, like the system itself, still
aren’t exciting, but put more simply: if

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.500
you are an American, regardless of whether
you use the Interstate system, your life would

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.679
be noticeably different in a reality without
the Interstates.

00:01:08.679 --> 00:01:14.780
It’s a single collection of roadways, with
an influence so great that it permeates into

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.340
every American’s life.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.189
It all started here, 102 years ago.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:28.399
79 vehicles departed from the White House’s
Ellipse at 11:15 AM on July 7th, 1919.

00:01:28.399 --> 00:01:32.509
They snaked through the District’s streets
before crossing into Maryland and stopping

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.380
for lunch in Rockville at 12:30 PM.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:39.840
The coupling on one of their kitchen trailers
broke and a car’s fan belt too, but all

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.200
in all, it was an uneventful 7 hour, 46 mile,
74 kilometer journey to the Frederick Fair

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.200
Grounds.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.069
12 and a half hours later, they woke up, left,
drove, arrived, and slept again.

00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:55.459
This cycle repeated itself.

00:01:55.459 --> 00:02:01.310
Day after day, they cut 40 or 50 or 60 miles
deeper into their westward journey.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.499
The convoy climbed through the heights of
the Appalachian mountains, trekked through

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:11.090
the mud of Ohio, before crossing the Mississippi
River at 4:10 PM on July 22nd.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:15.530
From there, they entered an area defined by
desolation.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:20.990
Few dared venture west in a motor vehicle
in 1919—always opting for the relative comfort

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.670
and speed of passenger trains.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.600
This convoy, though, was making the journey
specifically to determine just how difficult

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:35.490
this feat was—to what extent such a journey
was feasible for military logistics purposes.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.490
The answer came quickly.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:43.130
Despite routing themselves on the most developed
transcontinental road of the time, the Lincoln

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.740
Highway, they constantly had to divert around
disrepair, physically disassemble covered

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.570
bridges to get their trucks through, and fight
physics on roads too narrow for an oncoming

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.670
car to pass.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:03:00.490
Gas stations were few and far between, so
such travel required truly expedition-level

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.490
planning.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.410
Each time they entered a city, though, they
were greeted by hoards of intrigued locals

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:10.780
and dignitaries, but those crowds thinned
as the convoy crawled further into the desolate

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:11.780
west.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.100
Most, but certainly not all, of their vehicles
successfully traversed the Rockies, and the

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.230
same can be said for their trip across some
of the Lincoln Highway’s roughest roads

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.500
in Utah.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.610
With time, though, the trend reversed, roads
improved, and they finally saw pavement again

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.390
as the convoy entered California—its final
state.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:36.560
After a final evening in Oakland, California,
the vehicles loaded onto a ferry, crossed

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.450
the bay, and made a ceremonious arrival in
San Francisco accompanied by a massive parade

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.910
and a formal dinner.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:52.530
That was September 6, 1919—62 days after
their departure from the White House Ellipse.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.780
Having concentrated nearly all their waking
hours on driving, the math works out to an

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:02.810
average travel speed of just 5.65 miles or
9 kilometers per hour—a staggeringly slow

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.720
pace on a road branded as a highway, in a
year featuring technologies such as electric

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.530
refrigerators, airplanes, and long-distance
phone service.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:15.970
The convoy accomplished its mission of appraising
the feasibility of this route for more practical

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:21.140
military purposes, but the report back to
Washington was not positive.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:26.430
One Lieutenant Colonel, tasked with observing
the journey, wrote, “Extended trips by trucks

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:31.180
through the middle western part of the United
States are impractical until the roads are

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.870
improved, and then only a light truck should
be used on long hauls.”

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:41.030
It was clear that, for now, until roads were
improved, transcontinental motor vehicle trips

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.930
would remain the domain of publicity stunts
and enthusiasts.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.870
But then the years started passing.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:53.440
Administration after administration, observing
the growing presence of motor vehicles in

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.330
the lives of increasingly everyday Americans,
recognized that road construction was not

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.460
something that could be left the responsibility
of states.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.230
While individual states did make plenty of
progress building high quality urban and rural

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.050
roads, their interests were insular.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:13.820
Iowa, for example, would have no interest
building a highway with the primary purpose

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:16.290
of connecting Illinois to Nebraska.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.380
As Americans went off to fight in Europe during
World War Two, they observed the efficiency

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.090
of Germany’s national Reichsautobahn highway
system, but when they returned home, they

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.780
were forced to navigate a patchwork of state
systems of varying quality to get anywhere

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.340
beyond their local areas.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:37.470
In the 1950s, when America was a mere decade
out from landing men on the moon, it still

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:42.990
took significant planning and the better part
of 10 days to reasonably traverse from one

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.470
coast to the other.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:48.990
While this might have been inconvenient for
road-tripping Americans, it mostly meant that

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:54.260
there were regions that simply could not compete
with coastal areas in the industrial sector

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.240
due to high transport costs and times.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.420
It was clear something had to change, but
efforts to increase highway construction at

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:07.340
the federal level were constantly stalled
by funding issues and a general lack of volition.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.470
But then came someone new.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.700
One of the members of 1919 transcontinental
convoy, in fact, the very Lieutenant Colonel

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:20.760
who provided the earlier report on the journey
to Washington, continued on in his military

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.320
career, and rose through the ranks until he
became Supreme Allied Commander of the European

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.440
theatre in World War Two.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.070
Then, upon return to the US, he entered a
political career, culminating with his inauguration

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:37.590
as president on January 20, 1953.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:42.340
This was, of course, Dwight D Eisenhower.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.110
With the groundwork laid by decades of planning
and false starts, Eisenhower’s task was

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:52.720
centered on finally finding the political
will and funding to turn this concept map,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.479
a 1955 publication by the Bureau of Public
Roads, into reality.

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:02.000
After nearly three years of political maneuvering,
he succeeded.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:10.100
On June 29th, the Federal-Aid Highway Act
of 1956 was signed—dedicating some $25 billion

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:15.830
to constructing 41,000 miles or 66,000 kilometers
of highway.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:20.580
Unlike previous attempts at similar programs,
the federal government would now pay 90% of

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:26.199
the costs of these Interstate highways, with
individual states responsible for the rest—largely

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:29.120
funded by a hike in the federal gas tax.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.430
Just three months after the bill’s signing,
paving work began on a section of Interstate

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:39.640
70 in Kansas, and two months after that, the
same section opened to the public—representing

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:45.010
the first miles of a system that would engulf
the country over the coming decades.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:51.580
Now, whether it’s I-40 in Tennessee or I-90
through Idaho, the way these roads were built

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:55.120
was highly, highly standardized.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:58.009
That was, after all, the point.

00:07:58.009 --> 00:08:03.090
These standards are codified in a simple eight
page document entitled “A Policy on Design

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.270
Standards—Interstate System.”

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:11.160
To start with, fundamentally, Interstates
must have controlled access—meaning, the

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.850
manner in which vehicles enter or exit the
roadway must be controlled through on and

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:16.850
off ramps.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:21.490
That’s why, unlike with many state highways,
you never see intersections with stoplights

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:25.290
on Interstates, except when rules are violated.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:30.550
The standards also set out a minimum speed—50
miles or 80 kilometers per hour or in urban

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:37.320
and mountainous areas, 70 miles or 110 kilometers
per hour in rural areas—and maximum grade—up

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:43.910
to 7%, corresponding to 370 feet of elevation
gain per mile of road, or 70 meters per kilometer,

00:08:43.910 --> 00:08:46.290
depending on speed and topography.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:51.630
There must be at least two lanes in each direction
of travel, 12 feet or 3.5 meters in width,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:56.530
with a cross slope—essentially, an angle
to allow for rain run off—of at least 1.5%

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:58.980
and ideally 2% grade.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.550
Tunnels and bridges must have at least 16
feet or 5 meters of vertical clearance, although

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:08.399
less is allowed through urban areas as long
as an alternate route with 16 foot clearance

00:09:08.399 --> 00:09:09.870
is available.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:15.070
Aside from a few smaller details, that’s
the entirety of the Interstate highway design

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:16.070
standards.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.160
It’s incredibly simple.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.080
Implementing this nationwide, however, was
no simple endeavor.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:29.700
When the project started, in the 50’s, one
mile of Interstate cost some $8.5 million

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:35.680
to construct, or $5.3 million per kilometer,
however, through the decades, as labor costs

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:41.290
rose and the simplest sections stood completed,
this ramped up to over $34 million per mile,

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.960
or $21 million per kilometer.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:50.260
Unsurprisingly, the Interstate system quickly
blew past its original $25 billon budget allocation,

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:55.350
but people and governments were convinced—the
project gained more and more funding, and

00:09:55.350 --> 00:10:00.080
was reaching completion by the time the 80s
turned into the 90s.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:07.360
To complete the original vision, just one
last section remained: a 12.5 mile, 20 kilometer

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.040
stretch through Glenwood Canyon, Colorado.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:14.600
With thousand foot walls flanking on each
side, little room exists aside the Colorado

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.380
River for a roadway, but a complex collection
of bridges and tunnels were constructed at

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:27.300
a cost of $960 million, inflation adjusted,
corresponding to $75 million per mile or $47

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:30.070
million per kilometer of roadway.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:35.260
Once this engineering marvel was completed
in 1992, however, the nation was connected,

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:40.870
and more than two decades after his death,
Eisenhower’s vision was realized.

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:49.820
His 62-day transcontinental expedition from
1919 now takes a mere 42 hours.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:54.570
In such a decentralized country, consisting
of a patchwork of states each with unique

00:10:54.570 --> 00:11:00.490
governments, laws, cultures, and more, the
consistency of a nationwide, federal transportation

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.270
system is, in and of itself, unique.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:08.100
It is one of the most regular interactions
Americans, especially in far-flung western

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.090
and southern regions, might have with a federal
program.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:18.450
Today, the largest urban center located further
than 25 miles or 40 kilometers from an Interstate

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:23.220
highway is Fresno, California, with a population
of about half a million.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:30.600
In fact, only 14 cities with over 100,000
people are located further than this distance.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.420
Even Alaska, Hawaii, and Puerto Rico are now
included in this program, despite the fact

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:39.310
that their Interstates don’t actually extend
Interstate.

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:45.870
With wide lanes, smooth turns, consistent
grades, and more, these highways are remarkably

00:11:45.870 --> 00:11:46.870
safe.

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:52.600
While nationwide the US averages 1.3 deaths
per 100 million vehicle miles travelled, Interstates,

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:59.410
for the same 100 million vehicle miles, observe
just 0.55 deaths each year.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:05.050
Incredibly, that means that, assuming the
alternative is equivalent travel on non-Interstate

00:12:05.050 --> 00:12:11.550
roads, this system is responsible for saving
about 6,500 lives per year.

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:16.670
Beyond the moral component—the innate human
desire to preserve life—the US Department

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:22.170
of Transportation, on a statistical basis,
values a human life at $11.7 million dollars

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:26.611
when evaluating potential safety standards,
so the safety profile of the Interstate Highway

00:12:26.611 --> 00:12:33.000
system by itself has a theoretical economic
impact of about $75 billion per year—therefore

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.310
easily justifying its cost alone.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:39.040
But then there’s the more direct impacts.

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:43.110
Among the more populated states, construction
disproportionately took place in southern

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:48.330
states such as Georgia, South Carolina, or
Arkansas on a basis of miles constructed per

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:49.330
capita.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:54.300
Then, in less populous states, the Interstate
disproportionately penetrated the American

00:12:54.300 --> 00:13:00.140
west, with states such as Utah, Montana, Nevada,
and Wyoming receiving the most miles per capita.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:05.390
Therefore, it was the South and West that
received the greatest impact.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:11.120
In essence, what these roadways did was connect
the US’ economic centers to the periphery,

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:15.870
so the periphery had the most to gain by,
effectively, reducing the relative distance

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:19.410
to centers by a factor of 30%, on average.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.260
In the case of Interstate 5, for example,
which runs along the entire west coast from

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:29.029
the border to Mexico to that with Canada,
California, the state that encompasses the

00:13:29.029 --> 00:13:33.339
majority of its distance, actually sees the
smallest benefit.

00:13:33.339 --> 00:13:37.899
That’s not to say that benefit is small—the
removal of the Interstate would correspond

00:13:37.899 --> 00:13:44.079
to a 6% loss in overall income in the state—but
Oregon, the 12th most sparsely populated state

00:13:44.079 --> 00:13:49.680
in the nation, would lose 15% of its income
if I-5 simply disappeared.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:58.250
It’s worth fully considering just how staggering
that statistic is—a single, simple four-lane

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:04.770
roadway, stretching some 300 miles or 500
kilometers through Oregon, attributable for

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.730
15% of its income.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:11.279
This does, however, make sense.

00:14:11.279 --> 00:14:15.700
Oregon’s economy—significantly undergirded
by the natural resources, manufacturing, and

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:19.339
agriculture sectors—is highly trade dependent.

00:14:19.339 --> 00:14:25.610
The state does not, however, have any major
export ports, so when a manufacturer in Salem,

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:30.399
Oregon, for example, needs to transport finished
products to the port of Oakland, it would

00:14:30.399 --> 00:14:35.070
likely hire a truck to take the 9-hour journey
south on I-5.

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:39.870
The shortest non-Interstate route, however,
would take 13 and a half hours on roads with

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:45.380
potentially burdensome weight restrictions,
inadequate facilities, low bridges, and other

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.380
constraints.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:49.130
It just makes getting product out harder.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:55.250
Now, ultimately, transport cost is rarely
an enormous portion of a given product’s

00:14:55.250 --> 00:15:00.370
cost—a five-cent increase on a $10 product
produced further from Oakland isn’t massive—but

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:05.810
that’s not the context that matters in the
competition between places for industry.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:10.850
If the options are between a state that has
a conduit enabling lower-cost, reliable, fast

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:15.990
transportation in and out, and all other factors
are the same, why would a manufacturer decide

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.420
to locate is the more poorly connected place?

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:25.080
The economy is a competition, and in competition,
the smallest margins matter, even when they

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:28.320
don’t in a non-competitive context.

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:33.120
Considering the alternate was the status quo—individual
states building their own highways, disconnected

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:37.800
from any national program—this program caught
the states that couldn’t afford massive

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:42.760
infrastructure projects, many of which were
in the South and West, up to the rest.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:48.389
Therefore, Interstates made these states more
competitive by reducing their relative distance

00:15:48.389 --> 00:15:50.180
to the rest of the world.

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:54.290
That’s not to say the program was perfect,
though.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:16:00.370
Just as much as it proved revolutionary to
more rural areas, it gutted cities.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:05.730
Neighborhoods, predominantly poorer ones,
were leveled to the ground to make space for

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:09.120
Interstates and their associated infrastructure.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:14.220
This often created clear, physical barriers
between certain neighborhoods and urban centers,

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:17.360
which reduced opportunity in these excluded
neighborhoods.

00:16:17.360 --> 00:16:22.450
They also had the effect of encouraging reduced
density, which increases the environmental

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:27.140
impact of cities, creates scenarios where
job opportunity is linked to car ownership,

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:30.120
and additional potential negative impacts.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:36.970
Altogether, the way in which Interstates interact
with cities was often poorly planned and executed.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:42.760
But when examined in aggregate, the Interstate
highway system is one of the best investments

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:45.170
the United States ever made.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:50.829
While by no means unique to the United States
in concept, the vastness and regional disparities

00:16:50.829 --> 00:16:56.060
in the country meant such a system had a uniquely
powerful impact.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:17:02.390
For every dollar spent on the program, it
has returned at least six in economic benefit.

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:06.510
In the context of nations, connectivity is
power.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:11.390
Therefore, the reason why the Interstate highway
system is the most impactful infrastructure

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:18.170
program in American history is simply because,
as of now, it’s the largest.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:23.660
I definitely enjoy nerding out about the Interstates,
and hopefully you now do too, so I made a

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:29.299
whole other video about the strangest, quirkiest
sections of the system, like why Interstate

00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:34.750
180 exists—just connecting to a town of
700, and travelled on by only about 100 vehicles

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:40.419
per hour—or the town that essentially exists
because of one of the only stoplights on an

00:17:40.419 --> 00:17:41.419
Interstate.

00:17:41.419 --> 00:17:46.230
You can watch that video on Nebula, the streaming
site founded by myself and loads of your favorite

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:47.820
educational creators.

